\section{Introduction}

Most clinical trials assess treatment effects in a single patient subgroup.
However, new clinical trials often treat patients with the same disease but
different genetic profiles in multiple subgroups. To address this challenge, a
new statistical methodology is proposed for basket trials to optimize patient
recruitment and enhance accuracy in treatment evaluation. Two strategies are
developed for sharing information between groups: the "pooling" method and
the "proportional" method.

\section{Methods}

Two types of Bayesian approaches, "fixed" and "recursive", are used to calculate the required number of patients for each subgroup. The "proportional" method is also employed to share information between groups and two scenarios are examined: the "full" scenario where data is combined and the "selective" scenario where only significant results are shared.

\section{Results}

Theoretical studies validate our approaches and demonstrate better performance in terms of accuracy and recruitment efficiency compared to traditional methods that do not allow information sharing. Finally, a practical example is presented to illustrate the benefits of our proposed strategies.

\section{Discussion}

Two types of Bayesian approaches, "fixed" and "recursive", are used to calculate the required number of patients for each subgroup. The "proportional" method is also employed to share information between groups and two scenarios are examined: the "full" scenario where data is combined and the "selective" scenario where only significant results are shared.

\section{Conclusion}

The "proportional" method with shared information results in improved accuracy and increased efficiency in terms of required patients compared to traditional methods that do not allow information sharing.

